<TopicPage slug="subjects/engineering/foundational/statics-structures">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Engineering is design under constraint: physics sets the rules, money sets the budget, and safety
    sets the floor. The oldest constraint is <strong>statics</strong> — the study of things that must
    not move. Every structure, from a chair to a suspension bridge, is a machine for
    <strong>guiding forces into the ground</strong> without any part exceeding what it can carry. That
    sentence hides the discipline's two halves, which this lesson walks in order: <em>statics</em>
    works out what forces each part must carry (equilibrium, free-body diagrams, trusses), and
    <em>strength of materials</em> works out whether the part survives carrying them (stress, strain,
    bending, buckling, failure). Neither half is optional: a perfect force analysis of an inadequate
    beam still falls down, and an over-designed beam of unknown forces is guesswork with a budget.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="a part under load — Hooke's law against its yield limit; the crossing is where it fails" widget="plotter" config={hookeCfg} />

  <p>
    Hooke's law (1676) says stretch is proportional to load — until it isn't. Slide the stiffness k
    and watch where the load line meets the red yield limit: the extension at failure. Real engineers
    never let that point be reachable — they multiply expected loads by a <strong>safety factor</strong>
    (bridges ~1.5–2 on live loads, aircraft structure ~1.5, cranes much higher) so the failure zone
    sits far outside anything the real world delivers. Keep this picture: every calculation below
    exists to place a point safely to the left of that crossing.
  </p>

  <h2>Forces as vectors: the statics toolkit</h2>
  <p>
    A force has magnitude, direction and point of application — it is a <strong>vector</strong>, and
    the vector language is where all statics starts. Forces add tip-to-tail; any set of forces can be
    reduced to a single <strong>resultant</strong> plus a moment. <strong>Force systems</strong> come
    in the standard varieties: <em>concurrent</em> (all lines of action through one point — no net
    moment from them), <em>parallel</em>, and <em>distributed</em> (a load spread over an area, like
    snow on a roof or water on a dam, handled by its resultant acting at the centroid). Rotation
    needs its own quantity: the <strong>moment</strong> of a force, M = F × d (force times
    perpendicular distance), and a <strong>couple</strong> — two equal, opposite, non-collinear forces
    — produces pure rotation with zero resultant force, which is why a screwdriver turns the screw
    without shoving it across the table. Units matter more than beginners believe, and the syllabus's
    unit topic exists because errors here are how bridges fail on paper: mass (kg) versus force (N =
    kg × g) versus the US engineering menagerie (lbm the mass, lbf the force, joined by the constant
    g<sub>c</sub>; the slug, the dyne, the ton) — a weight calculation must convert mass to force
    explicitly, and 1 kg under Earth's gravity weighs about 9.81 N. The first habit of any engineer:
    before calculating anything, estimate. Is this a tonne problem or a gram problem? A quick
    Fermi estimate catches more design errors than any software.
  </p>

  <h2>Free-body diagrams and equilibrium</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>free-body diagram (FBD)</strong> is statics' core ritual: isolate one body, cut it
    free from the world, and draw <em>every</em> force acting on it — applied loads, its weight, and
    the <strong>support reactions</strong> (rollers push perpendicular; pins push in two directions;
    fixed supports also resist moment). Then demand balance: <strong>ΣF = 0 and ΣM = 0</strong> — two
    or three equations per body, solved for the unknowns. Worked example: a 6 m simply supported beam
    carries a 600 N load 2 m from the left support. Take moments about the left support:
    R<sub>R</sub> × 6 = 600 × 2, so the right reaction is 200 N; vertical balance gives the left
    reaction 400 N. Notice the load did <em>not</em> split evenly — it sits closer to one end, and the
    reactions know it. Ninety per cent of statics is this ritual done honestly: the discipline is
    completeness (a missed force makes the arithmetic lie confidently) and the equations do the rest.
    When a system has more unknowns than equilibrium equations, it is <strong>statically
    indeterminate</strong> — extra equations must come from the structure's stiffness (how much each
    support stretches), which is the point where statics hands off to strength of materials.
  </p>

  <h2>Internal forces: stress, strain and trusses</h2>
  <p>
    Equilibrium finds the external reactions; cutting a member open reveals its <strong>internal
    forces</strong>. A <strong>truss</strong> — the triangular lattice of bridges, cranes and roofs —
    is the clean case: members carry pure <strong>tension</strong> (being pulled) or pure
    <strong>compression</strong> (being squeezed), no bending, because loads meet only at joints.
    The <strong>method of joints</strong> applies the FBD ritual to each pin: ΣF = 0 in two directions
    walks the forces member by member. A triangle is rigid where a rectangle can shear into a
    parallelogram — geometry doing structural work, which is why triangles are the shape of load-bearing
    design. The intensity of the internal force is <strong>stress</strong>: σ = F/A (force over
    cross-sectional area, in pascals), split into normal stress (perpendicular to the cut — tension/
    compression) and <strong>shear stress</strong> (parallel to the cut — scissors and bolts). The
    response is <strong>strain</strong> ε = ΔL/L (fractional stretch), and in the elastic region
    stress and strain are proportional: σ = Eε, where E is Young's modulus — Hooke's law in its
    rigorous form, and the curve in the widget above. Everything is a spring: a steel tie of 1 m and
    100 mm² carrying 10 kN stretches δ = FL ÷ (EA) = 10,000 × 1 ÷ (200 × 10⁹ × 100 × 10⁻⁶) = 0.5 mm
    — visible in precision machinery, invisible in a bridge, but always there. <strong>Superposition
    (combined loading)</strong> says that for linear elastic members, several loads' effects simply
    add: a column that is compressed <em>and</em> bent has the stresses of each, summed — and the
    worst sum, not the worst single load, picks the failure.
  </p>

  <h2>Beams, bending and the diagrams</h2>
  <p>
    The beam is civilisation's workhorse member, and its analysis is the second great ritual. Draw
    the <strong>shear force diagram</strong> and the <strong>bending moment diagram</strong>: cut the
    beam at every station, ask what force and what moment the right half exerts on the left, and plot
    both along the length. The rules connect them — the shear diagram's slope is the load, the moment
    diagram's slope is the shear — and the beam fails where the moment peaks. For the 6 m beam above,
    the peak moment is 400 × 2 = 800 N·m at the load point. Bending stress follows
    <strong>σ = M·y ÷ I</strong>: the moment M, the distance y from the beam's neutral axis, and I —
    the <strong>second moment of area</strong>, the property that measures how the material is
    distributed around that axis. I is why the I-beam has its shape: the flanges carry most of the
    material <em>far</em> from the neutral axis, where bending stress has room to work, so an I-section
    resists bending many times better than the same amount of steel formed into a solid square
    (the parallel axis theorem and radius of gyration quantify exactly this game). Bending couples
    with transverse <strong>shear</strong> (the web of the I-beam's job), with <strong>torsion</strong>
    (twisting — τ = T·c ÷ J, the circular-shaft analogue of bending, driving drive-shaft design), and
    deflection (a beam's sag δ grows with load and length⁴ and shrinks with EI — which is why depth
    beats material when a beam sags). Columns fail differently: <strong>Euler buckling</strong>
    (P<sub>cr</sub> = π²EI ÷ L²) says a slender column can collapse sideways long before its material
    crushes — buckling is a stiffness-and-geometry failure, and it is why doubling a column's
    <em>length</em> quarters its capacity.
  </p>

  <h2>Friction, and the honest catalogue of failure</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Static friction</strong> is the third pillar of statics: contact surfaces resist sliding up
    to a limit F ≤ μN (the coefficient μ, times the normal force), then let go. Friction is why
    ladders stand (the wall is nearly frictionless; the floor's friction holds the whole business),
    why wedges and brakes work, and why slopes fail — a soil bank steeper than its friction angle
    slides. On the strength side, the failure catalogue deserves its own respect. <strong>Principal
    stresses and Mohr's circle</strong> transform an arbitrary stress state into the maximum tension,
    compression and shear any plane in the material feels; <strong>yield criteria</strong> turn that
    state into a verdict — <strong>Tresca</strong> (yield when maximum shear reaches the value found
    in a tension test) or <strong>von Mises</strong> (yield when a combined distortion-energy measure
    crosses the material's limit; the standard for ductile metals). Beyond yielding:
    <strong>fatigue</strong> — cracks growing under repeated loads far below the yield limit (the
    reason aircraft parts have inspection hours and bridges have fatigue ratings);
    <strong>creep</strong> — slow deformation under steady load at high temperature (turbine blades
    live their whole lives by creep schedules); <strong>brittle fracture</strong> — sudden crack
    propagation in cold or notch-sensitive materials (the WWII Liberty ships that snapped in Arctic
    water); and <strong>stress concentrations</strong> — a sharp corner or hole multiplying local
    stress by factors of several, which is why aircraft fuselage windows are oval and every
    load-bearing design rounds its corners. The <strong>factor of safety</strong> wraps the whole
    catalogue: working stress = material limit ÷ factor, with the factor sized to the uncertainty —
    known loads and certified steel get 1.5; unknown soils, repeated dynamic loads or human-suspension
    applications get 5 and up.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> strong and stiff are not the same. Strength is the load a part
  survives (yield limit); stiffness is how little it deflects under load (E, the slope). Rubber is
  flexible yet strong in tension; glass is stiff yet fragile. A design can fail by being too floppy
  while every stress is safe — the sagging bookshelf nobody sat on twice — and the fix is geometry
  (depth, I), not a stronger alloy.</p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The professional layer is about honesty with uncertainty. <strong>Tacoma Narrows (1940)</strong> —
    "Galloping Gertie" — collapsed four months after opening; the folklore says resonance, but the
    engineering finding is <strong>aeroelastic flutter</strong>: wind feeding energy into a coupled
    torsional-vertical oscillation until the deck tore itself apart. The lesson that survives in every
    modern wind code is structural: stiffness and damping are wind-resistance, and slender flexibility
    is not elegance but exposure. <strong>Codes embody accumulated failure</strong> (Petroski's thesis
    in <em>To Engineer Is Human</em>): every clause in a design code is a fossil of a specific
    disaster, and modern practice has shifted from the deterministic safety factor toward
    <strong>limit state design</strong> — partial factors on loads <em>and</em> materials separately,
    sized by probability, so that "safe" means "failure unlikely enough over the structure's life",
    which is the statistics subject's confidence interval wearing a hard hat. And the quiet frontier
    of statics is computation: the <strong>finite element method</strong> divides any shape into
    thousands of elements, applies exactly the equilibrium-and-Hooke's-law mathematics above, and
    returns colour maps of stress — but the colour map is only as honest as its boundary conditions,
    which is why the free-body diagram, the Fermi estimate and the habit of asking "what carries this
    load to the ground?" remain the irreplaceable core. Structures stand when every load has a
    complete, calculated path to the earth; everything in this lesson is the machinery for checking
    that path, one arrow at a time.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hooke (1676); Hibbeler, Engineering Mechanics: Statics and Mechanics of Materials; Gere & Goodno, Mechanics of Materials; Petroski, To Engineer Is Human (1985); Tacoma Narrows Bridge failure report (Amann et al., 1941).</p>
</TopicPage>
